INT. VICTORIAN CONSERVATORY - TEA TABLE - AFTERNOON

1887. Glass walls, steaming ferns, a wicker table set with a silver service. Rain streaks the panes.

AUGUSTUS FINCH, 50s, whiskers like frost, holds a teacup in a hand that does not shake. LAVINIA CROSSLEY, 40s, in grey silk, pours. BARNABY HOOLE, 30s, a young curate, perches on the edge of a chair. PRUDENCE LOVELACE, 20s, a governess, stands by the ferns with a book.

LAVINIA
Sugar, Mr. Finch?

AUGUSTUS
One lump.

LAVINIA
Barnaby?

BARNABY
Two, please.

PRUDENCE
(to herself)
Three, if I were permitted.

LAVINIA
I beg your pardon?

PRUDENCE
Nothing, ma'am.

The teaspoon clinks. Augustus sips. He sets down the cup, and the saucer rattles. He studies Barnaby. He studies Prudence. She meets his eyes and does not look away.

AUGUSTUS
Miss Lovelace. You are acquainted with the curate.

PRUDENCE
I am.

BARNABY
We met at the vicarage.

AUGUSTUS
And did you leave it together?

Silence. The rain intensifies. Barnaby shifts in his chair, Lavinia lifts the teapot, and Prudence closes her book, all three moving at once, like a stage pantomime, while Augustus smiles and spreads butter on a scone.

LAVINIA
(to the table)
Shall we have the lemon cake?

AUGUSTUS
By all means.

BARNABY
(quietly)
I should be going.

AUGUSTUS
Sit.

He sits. Prudence steps to the table. She picks up the knife and cuts the cake into perfect, even slices.

PRUDENCE
It would be a pity to waste it.

Augustus chuckles. Lavinia relaxes by a hair.
